Chapter: FatWritePhases

  cites Works chapter GopFat16
  cites Works chapter GopRtc
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16

 How to run it, because nothing else does:

   build/compile.ps1 -Src docs/Probes/fat-write-phases.codex `
     -Out out.cdx -Kernel seed/Codex.cdx -Log out.log
   Copy-Item seed/Codex.img scratch.img          # it WRITES, use a copy
   Set-ItemProperty scratch.img IsReadOnly $false  # the copy inherits Perforce's read-only bit
   tools/codex-vm.exe -kernel out.cdx -output out.txt -mem 3072 `
     -headless -disk scratch.img

 It is not in the battery: it needs a 16 MB disk. It took about a minute when
 it was written, nearly all of it the write it exists to time; measured
 2026-08-20 against the string data phase it is 3.3 s, and the phases it
 decomposes now all report 0 s at the RTC's one-second resolution. The
 IDE CENSUS line is what still carries a number worth reading.

 Read the read-only line above rather than skipping it. Without it every
 write-back is refused, the guest still reports the save complete, and the
 run comes back about ten times FASTER, which reads as a good number rather
 than as a missing one. codex-vm now says so on stderr and counts the
 refusals in its IDE CENSUS line; check that line before quoting a time.

 WORKS-18 asked which of the three phases of a big save dominates and said
 plainly that it was unmeasured. This calls the same phases in the same order
 that gfat-write-file-in calls them, so the decomposition is faithful by
 construction rather than by a separate control.

 The clock is the CMOS RTC read as hours, minutes and seconds, which spans a
 day at one-second resolution. The HPET was tried first and faults: a
 peek-32 of 0xFED000F0 kills the guest in a plain bare-metal program.

 Every component is range-checked and the read retried, and one read is
 taken and discarded before anything is timed. Without both, the self-test
 below reported 264,469 seconds for three seconds of waiting.

Section: The Clock

  clock-fuel : Integer = 200

  clock-try : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  clock-try (d) = act
    idle <- rtc-wait-idle rtc-uip-fuel
    b <- rtc-read rtc-reg-status-b
    ss <- rtc-read 0
    mm <- rtc-read 2
    hh <- rtc-read 4
    let bin = bit-and b rtc-binary-bit
    in let s = rtc-decode ss bin
    in let m = rtc-decode mm bin
    in let h = rtc-decode hh bin
    in if idle == 0 then 0 - 1
    else if s < 0 then 0 - 1
    else if s > 59 then 0 - 1
    else if m < 0 then 0 - 1
    else if m > 59 then 0 - 1
    else if h < 0 then 0 - 1
    else if h > 23 then 0 - 1
    else h * 3600 + m * 60 + s
  end

  clock-secs : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  clock-secs (fuel) = act
    v <- clock-try 0
    if v >= 0 then v
    else if fuel <= 0 then 0 - 1
    else clock-secs (fuel - 1)
  end

  wait-edge : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  wait-edge (s) = act
    n <- clock-secs clock-fuel
    if n /= s then n else wait-edge s
  end

 An instrument that cannot fail is not evidence. Three edges must read as
 three seconds; anything else and the numbers below mean nothing.

  self-test : Integer -> [Device.Port] Integer
  self-test (d) = act
    a <- clock-secs clock-fuel
    b <- wait-edge a
    c <- wait-edge b
    e <- wait-edge c
    e - a
  end

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Device.Port, Device.Block] Nothing
  opening = act
    warm <- clock-secs clock-fuel
    st <- self-test 0
    print-line-uni ("clock self-test: 3 edges read " & show st & " s (3 is correct)")
    if st /= 3 then print-line-uni "INSTRUMENT FAILED -- numbers below mean nothing"
    else print-line-uni ""
    m <- gfat-mount-esp
    if m.gm-ok == False then print-line-uni "MOUNT failed"
    else act
      let vol = m.gm-vol
      in let size = 2896050
      in let cbytes = (vol.vol-bytes-per-sector) * (vol.vol-sectors-per-cluster)
      in let need = (size + cbytes - 1) / cbytes
      in let src = alloc-zeroed size 64
      in act
        print-line-uni ("size=" & show size & " cluster-bytes=" & show cbytes & " clusters-needed=" & show need & " fat-sectors=" & show (vol.vol-fat-sectors))
        t0 <- clock-secs clock-fuel
        f <- gfat-load-fat vol
        t1 <- clock-secs clock-fuel
        if f.gft-ok == False then print-line-uni "LOAD-FAT failed"
        else let first = gfat-chain-alloc vol (f.gft-buf) need 2 0 0
        in act
          t2 <- clock-secs clock-fuel
          if first == 0 then print-line-uni "CHAIN-ALLOC no space"
          else act
            clear <- gfat-chain-is-clear vol (f.gft-buf) first need
            t3 <- clock-secs clock-fuel
            wr <- gfat-write-runs vol (f.gft-buf) first ((size + 511) / 512) src 0
            t4 <- clock-secs clock-fuel
            ff <- gfat-flush-fat vol (f.gft-buf)
            t5 <- clock-secs clock-fuel
            print-line-uni ("load-fat    " & show (t1 - t0) & " s")
            print-line-uni ("chain-alloc " & show (t2 - t1) & " s")
            print-line-uni ("chain-clear " & show (t3 - t2) & " s")
            print-line-uni ("write-runs  " & show (t4 - t3) & " s")
            print-line-uni ("flush-fat   " & show (t5 - t4) & " s")
            print-line-uni ("TOTAL       " & show (t5 - t0) & " s")
            print-line-uni ("clear=" & show clear & " sectors-written=" & show wr & " of " & show ((size + 511) / 512) & " flush=" & show ff)
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end
